# Transcriptional response of a fictional cell line to a fictional compound — analysis code

This repository accompanies the synthetic manuscript *Transcriptional response of a
fictional cell line to a fictional compound*. Nothing in it describes a real study:
the cell line (SYNTH-CL1), the compound (SYNX-7), the reference (<%= reference_release %>),
the genes (SYN0001–SYN<%= format('%04d', gene_count) %>) and the reads are generated.
It exists so that reproducibility checks can be run against a study whose correct
answer is known in advance.

## Layout

```
config/config.yaml    paths, adapter, thresholds, permutation seed
config/samples.tsv    six samples, two conditions
data/                 reference (<%= reference_release %>) and single-end reads
envs/rnaseq.yml       the conda environment, versions pinned
envs/rnaseq.lock      conda-lock output for linux-64 (package URLs with checksums)
Dockerfile            the image the published results were produced in
Snakefile             trim -> salmon_index -> salmon_quant -> tximport -> deseq2 (+ QC report)
scripts/              tximport.R, deseq2.R
```

## Running

The results in the manuscript were produced in the Docker image built from this
repository, with one core:

```sh
docker build --platform linux/amd64 -t synthetic-rnaseq:1.0 .
docker run --rm --platform linux/amd64 -v "$PWD":/work -w /work synthetic-rnaseq:1.0 \
  snakemake --cores 1
```

Each rule also names a per-tool container by digest, for users who run Snakemake
with Apptainer instead (`snakemake --cores 1 --software-deployment-method apptainer`).
The published results come from the Docker route above.

The output is `results/synthetic_supplementary_table_S1.tsv` (Supplementary Table S1).
Intermediate files, tool reports and logs are written under `.work/`.

## Data

The reads and the reference are synthetic. They can be regenerated byte for byte
with the GenomicsChain self-application generator, seed <%= seed %>.
